# Commit gate. `pnpm check` is the full suite (lifecycle + docs + site + plugin
# tests + packaging + market index); development-time work uses `pnpm lint`
# instead, so this only ever runs once per commit.
#
# Husky v9 runs this file with sh and does not need a shebang or husky.sh sourcing.

# THE STALE-INDEX TRAP, and why the gate adopts a file it is about to judge.
#
# index.json is generated content owned by the release workflow: that workflow
# pushes `chore: record npm releases and plugin market index` while you are
# working, and `site:market:check` compares the committed copy byte-for-byte
# against a fresh npm reading. So a commit prepared before that push lands is
# judged against an index this commit never touched — and because a rebase needs
# a clean tree, there is no way forward: commit is blocked, rebasing is blocked.
#
# When HEAD is simply BEHIND origin/main, the remote copy is the workflow's own
# authority and adopting it changes nothing about what this commit proposes. A
# commit that stages (or has committed) its own index.json edit keeps it: there
# the local copy is the newer statement, and the check must judge that instead.
#
# Deliberately NO `git fetch` here: this reads the origin/main already known to
# the repository. Fetching inside a commit hook would add network latency and a
# failure mode to every commit, and the sync step documented in AGENTS.md is
# where the ref gets refreshed.
if git rev-parse --verify -q origin/main >/dev/null 2>&1 &&
  ! git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/main HEAD 2>/dev/null &&
  git diff --quiet origin/main...HEAD -- index.json 2>/dev/null &&
  git diff --cached --quiet -- index.json 2>/dev/null; then
  git checkout origin/main -- index.json && git add index.json
fi

pnpm check